diff --git a/src/core/vec.npk b/src/core/vec.npk
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c1b02da
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/core/vec.npk
@@ -0,0 +1,248 @@
+mod:vec;
+
+// `src/core/vec.npk` -- `Vec<T>`, `nsockets`' growable array (`meta/specs/BUILD.md` B-13, SK-005).
+//
+// MAY IMPORT: nothing (`meta/specs/BUILD.md` B-18's table); `core` is the bottom layer, and this file has no `use` line.
+//
+// The compiler's `List<T>` in shape -- a block, a count, a capacity, doubling -- and this library's own, because a
+// library must not import a compiler's internals. The block is `wild`: its lifetime is its owner's scope, so every
+// scope that makes a `Vec` calls `vec_free` on every path, or the program's `exit 0` traps `WildLeak` (D-151) -- which
+// is the enforcement wanted, and `tests/unit/vec_leak_forgotten_free.npk` keeps it measured.
+//
+// ---- AN INDEX THROUGH `items` IS NOT CHECKED, SO THIS FILE CHECKS EVERY ONE -----------------------------------------
+//
+// `items` is a `wild T->`, a bare pointer, and a pointer carries no length: an index through it reads or writes
+// whatever is there and answers (`meta/specs/SAFETY.md` §1's indexing row, @@PD-177@@). So every index and every count is
+// checked in the function that uses it, before the pointer is touched, and `items` is `hidden`, so no other module can
+// index it at all (NITPICK-TYPE-080). The sign is half of every check: `i < v.count` admits a negative `i`, so each
+// accessor checks `i >= 0` as well.
+//
+// ---- A MISUSE TRAPS THE LANGUAGE'S `OutOfBounds` --------------------------------------------------------------------
+//
+// An index out of range, a pop of an empty `Vec`, a negative capacity or count, a request whose byte size no `int64`
+// holds, and any use of a `Vec` after `vec_free` -- each is the caller's mistake, and each stops the program through
+// `vec_oob`, the language's own `OutOfBounds` (exit 94 in this repository's `failsafe` convention). Not a `Result`:
+// these functions are `never fails`, so a consumer's loop threads no error channel; and not an identity of this
+// library's, whose four are `meta/specs/SAFETY.md` §3's and none of them `core`'s. `vec_oob` indexes a one-element
+// fixed array, which carries its length, at -1 or -2 -- out of range for every argument -- so it cannot return:
+// `nitpick-regex`'s RX-143 found a stop that indexed at `i` returning for `i == 0`, the index every guard of an EMPTY
+// container passes.
+//
+// Every function's obligation is a COMMENT, in the exact syntax it would take (`meta/specs/VERIFICATION.md` Z-1a), with
+// a unit standing in: at the pin a live `requires` or `ensures` adds `RequiresViolated` or `EnsuresViolated` to every
+// consumer's bill, and a live `requires` would trap before the body's own check could, changing the identity a misuse
+// stops with. The comment is the clause; the checks in the body are what run.
+//
+// ---- A FREED `Vec` IS DEAD, AND EVERY VERB TRAPS ON ONE -------------------------------------------------------------
+//
+// `vec_free` leaves `cap == 0`, `count == 0` and `items == NULL` -- a `wild` pointer, where `NULL` stays legal (the
+// compiler's D-355). A `Vec` this file made otherwise always has `cap >= 1` (`vec_init` floors a request of 0 to 1),
+// so `cap <= 0` means freed, and every function here that is handed a `Vec` checks it FIRST: a push or a reserve would
+// otherwise grow a freed `Vec` from `ralloc(NULL, n)` -- a fresh block, and a use after free that succeeds in silence
+// -- and a second `vec_free` would reach `dalloc(NULL)`.
+//
+// ---- GROWTH DOUBLES, AND NO MULTIPLY CAN OVERFLOW -------------------------------------------------------------------
+//
+// Plain `int64` arithmetic traps on overflow (D-210), so every multiply is guarded BEFORE it runs. The doubling
+// (`vec_grown`) takes `want` itself when doubling would pass `int64`'s maximum; the byte count (`vec_bytes`) is
+// computed in `int128`, where two non-negative `int64`s cannot overflow a product, and is refused through `vec_oob`
+// when it does not fit an `int64`; and a reservation's `count + need` is refused before it is added. A byte count that
+// fits an `int64` but that no allocator can give is the allocator's own trap: `HeapBadRequest` past the runtime's 2^47
+// ceiling, `HeapOom` when memory runs out. Every loop here is a `for` over a range, never a `while`, so none needs a
+// `decreases` measure and none charges a consumer `DecreasesViolated`.
+//
+// ---- WHAT A `Vec` MAY HOLD, AND WHY IT IS MOVE-ONLY ----------------------------------------------------------------
+//
+// `T: Copy`, at the type and at every function (the compiler requires the bound repeated): an element owns nothing,
+// so every read is a copy, `vec_at` returns by value, and freeing the block is the whole free -- no element drop to
+// forget. An owning `T` is refused wherever it is written. The address types derive `Copy` (`meta/specs/
+// ADDRESS_MODEL.md` §1), so a `Vec<SocketAddr>` is one. The last field, `hidden string[0]:move_only`, holds nothing and
+// occupies nothing -- a zero-length array of an owning type -- and makes the `Vec` itself an owning type: a copy of a
+// `Vec`, which would be a second handle on one block, is NITPICK-TYPE-046, and a transfer is `move(v)`. So a reader
+// takes its `Vec` by value, which is a loan and read-only, and everything that changes one takes `Vec<T>->`.
+// `count` and `cap` are `sealed`: read anywhere, written only here (NITPICK-TYPE-079). No field carries a
+// `limit<...>`, which would charge every consumer `LimitViolated`.
+//
+// Every generic function here is instantiated by `tests/unit/generic_instances.npk`, whose emission build step 2's
+// call-edge scan reads: a generic function no root instantiates is in no emission, so no other scan sees it (@@PD-177@@).
+
+// ---- the type --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+pub struct:Vec<T: Copy> = {
+    hidden wild T->:items;         // the block: `cap` slots, the first `count` live. WILD.
+    sealed int64:count;            // elements in use
+    sealed int64:cap;              // elements the block holds; 0 once freed
+    hidden string[0]:move_only;    // zero bytes, holds nothing -- and makes a `Vec` move-only
+};
+
+// `int64`'s maximum, the bound every guard below is computed against. It is spelled: the MAXIMUM is inside the
+// literal envelope (the minimum is not, NITPICK-LEX-004).
+fixed int64:I64_MAX = 9223372036854775807i64;
+
+// ---- the stop ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails ensures false
+//
+// THE ONE OUT-OF-RANGE STOP IN THIS FILE. `i` is the value that was out of range, kept so a call site says which; the
+// index is `-1 - (i & 1)`, -1 or -2, out of range for this array and for an array of any length, at every `i`.
+func:vec_oob = NIL(int64:i) never fails {
+    int64[1]:guard = [0i64];
+    int64:below = 0i64 - 1i64 - (i & 1i64);
+    discard(guard[below]);
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+// ---- the guarded arithmetic -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails requires n >= 0i64 ensures result >= 0i64
+//
+// `n` ELEMENTS' BYTES, or a stop when they do not fit an `int64`. Both factors are `int64`s at least zero, so their
+// `int128` product is below 2^126 and the multiply cannot overflow; the comparison is the guard, and the narrowing
+// below it is exact.
+func:vec_bytes<T: Copy> = int64(int64:n) never fails {
+    int128:wide = (n => int128) * (#size_of<T>() => int128);
+    if (wide > (I64_MAX => int128)) { drop vec_oob(n); }
+    pass wide =>! int64;
+};
+
+// obligation: never fails requires want >= 1i64 ensures result >= want
+//
+// THE CAPACITY THAT HOLDS `want`: `from` doubled until it does, and `want` itself if the next doubling would pass
+// `int64`'s maximum. The guard `nc > I64_MAX - nc` runs before every `nc * 2i64` and cannot overflow (`nc >= 0`). From
+// `nc >= 1`, at most sixty-two doublings bring `nc` to 2^62 or past it, where the guard fires, so the sixty-third
+// turn at the latest ends the loop in a `break`; the line after the loop makes the postcondition hold whatever it did.
+func:vec_grown = int64(int64:from, int64:want) never fails {
+    int64:nc = from;
+    if (nc < 1i64) { nc = 1i64; }
+    for (int64:k in 0i64...63i64) {
+        if (nc >= want) { break; }
+        if (nc > I64_MAX - nc) {
+            nc = want;
+            break;
+        }
+        nc = nc * 2i64;
+    }
+    if (nc < want) { nc = want; }
+    pass nc;
+};
+
+// ---- construction and destruction -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails requires cap >= 0i64
+//             ensures result.count == 0i64 && result.cap >= 1i64 && result.cap >= cap
+//
+// A NEGATIVE CAPACITY IS A MISUSE AND STOPS; ZERO IS FLOORED TO ONE. A request of 0 is the natural "no size hint", so
+// it builds a usable `Vec` rather than a trap; a negative one can only come from arithmetic gone wrong. The floor is
+// what keeps `cap >= 1` true of every live `Vec`, so that `cap <= 0` can mean freed.
+pub func:vec_init<T: Copy> = Vec<T>(int64:cap) never fails {
+    if (cap < 0i64) { drop vec_oob(cap); }
+    int64:n = cap;
+    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
+    wild int8->:mem = alloc(raw vec_bytes::<T>(n));
+    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: 0i64, cap: n, move_only: [] };
+};
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64
+//             ensures v.count == 0i64 && v.cap == 0i64 && v.items == NULL
+//
+// FREES THE BLOCK, WHICH IS THE WHOLE FREE: an element owns nothing (`T: Copy`). It leaves the `Vec` dead -- see the
+// header -- so a second `vec_free` stops here, before `dalloc`.
+pub func:vec_free<T: Copy> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
+    v.items = NULL;
+    v.count = 0i64;
+    v.cap = 0i64;
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+// ---- capacity ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 && need >= 0i64
+//             ensures v.cap >= v.count + need && v.count == old(v.count)
+//
+// ROOM FOR `need` MORE elements beyond `count`, by doubling. The elements move with the block: `ralloc` copies the old
+// block's bytes and frees it.
+pub func:vec_reserve<T: Copy> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:need) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    if (need < 0i64) { drop vec_oob(need); }
+    if (need > I64_MAX - v.count) { drop vec_oob(need); }
+    int64:want = v.count + need;
+    if (want <= v.cap) { pass NIL; }
+    int64:nc = raw vec_grown(v.cap, want);
+    wild int8->:bigger = ralloc(v.items =>! wild int8->, raw vec_bytes::<T>(nc));
+    v.items = bigger =>! wild T->;
+    v.cap = nc;
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+// ---- the checked accessor pair ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 && i >= 0i64 && i < v.count
+//
+// BY VALUE: `T: Copy`, so the read is a copy and the slot keeps its element. Its `Vec` is a loan -- read, never
+// changed -- so a consumer reads a `Vec` held in a field of its own struct without taking an address.
+pub func:vec_at<T: Copy> = T(Vec<T>:v, int64:i) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    if (i < 0i64) { drop vec_oob(i); }
+    if (i >= v.count) { drop vec_oob(i); }
+    pass v.items[i];
+};
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 && i >= 0i64 && i < v.count
+//             ensures v.count == old(v.count)
+pub func:vec_set<T: Copy> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i, T:x) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    if (i < 0i64) { drop vec_oob(i); }
+    if (i >= v.count) { drop vec_oob(i); }
+    v.items[i] = x;
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+// ---- adding and removing ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 ensures v.count == old(v.count) + 1i64
+pub func:vec_push<T: Copy> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, T:x) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    if (v.count >= v.cap) {
+        drop vec_reserve(v, 1i64);
+        // `vec_reserve`'s postcondition, checked where the write below relies on it.
+        if (v.count >= v.cap) { drop vec_oob(v.count); }
+    }
+    v.items[v.count] = x;
+    v.count = v.count + 1i64;
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 && v.count > 0i64
+//             ensures v.count == old(v.count) - 1i64
+//
+// THE LAST ELEMENT, by value. An empty `Vec` stops rather than answering a value nobody chose.
+pub func:vec_pop<T: Copy> = T(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    if (v.count <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.count); }
+    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
+    pass v.items[v.count];
+};
+
+// ---- bulk operations --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 && n >= 0i64
+//             ensures (n < old(v.count) && v.count == n) || (n >= old(v.count) && v.count == old(v.count))
+//
+// KEEP THE FIRST `n`: a truncation to `n >= count` changes nothing (it never grows). The capacity is kept.
+pub func:vec_truncate<T: Copy> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:n) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    if (n < 0i64) { drop vec_oob(n); }
+    if (n < v.count) { v.count = n; }
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+// obligation: never fails requires v.cap > 0i64 ensures v.count == 0i64
+//
+// The capacity is kept, so a `Vec` reused clears rather than reallocates.
+pub func:vec_clear<T: Copy> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
+    if (v.cap <= 0i64) { drop vec_oob(v.cap); }
+    v.count = 0i64;
+    pass NIL;
+};
